# Beam-width ceiling sweep — 2026-09-10

`DESIGN_PHASE_EXPLORATION_NOT_A_CLAIM`

No learner, training run, policy run, or loss value was used. The sealed design point remains the one-sided half-power angle `1.66°`. The other widths are diagnostics over newly generated physics, not changes to a sealed artefact.

## Direct answer

**Yes.** At `2.40°`, the fresh-run acceleration ceiling is `+8.866177%`. At `3.32°`, the coordination ceiling is `+11.506880%` and the acceleration ceiling is `+38.291632%`. Those are plainly larger than the roughly 1–2% control band. The `2.40°` coordination ceiling, `+3.365834%`, is only a modest increase; it is not the main affirmative result.

| One-sided width | Coordination: joint / first − 1 | Acceleration: first / actual 10 s − 1 | Traversal: first / best − 1 | Materially above control band? |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|:---|
| 1.66° control | +1.944795% | +1.840863% | +0.905261% | No |
| 2.40° | +3.365834% | +8.866177% | +1.448816% | **Yes, acceleration** |
| 3.32° | +11.506880% | +38.291632% | −2.028159% | **Yes, both ceilings** |

The larger ceiling is not the same thing as a better operating point. At `3.32°`, the first fixed point is only `9.840072 Mbit/J`, versus `13.430253 Mbit/J` at `1.66°`; its served-PHY and rate-target counts also fall. The `2.40°` point is the only sampled wider point that combines higher absolute first/joint EE and higher fixed-point/joint target attainment with a materially larger acceleration ceiling.

