@@ -0,0 +1,125 @@
+**DESIGN-PHASE EXPLORATION — 1.66°: coordination +1.944795% and acceleration +1.840863%; 2.40°: +3.365834% and +8.866177%; 3.32°: +11.506880% and +38.291632%; yes, the declared wider-beam grid materially lifts at least one ceiling above the 1.66° band.**
+
+# Beam-width ceiling sweep — 2026-09-10
+
+`DESIGN_PHASE_EXPLORATION_NOT_A_CLAIM`
+
+No learner, training run, policy run, or loss value was used. The sealed design point remains the one-sided half-power angle `1.66°`. The other widths are diagnostics over newly generated physics, not changes to a sealed artefact.
+
+## Direct answer
+
+**Yes.** At `2.40°`, the fresh-run acceleration ceiling is `+8.866177%`. At `3.32°`, the coordination ceiling is `+11.506880%` and the acceleration ceiling is `+38.291632%`. Those are plainly larger than the roughly 1–2% control band. The `2.40°` coordination ceiling, `+3.365834%`, is only a modest increase; it is not the main affirmative result.
+
+| One-sided width | Coordination: joint / first − 1 | Acceleration: first / actual 10 s − 1 | Traversal: first / best − 1 | Materially above control band? |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|:---|
+| 1.66° control | +1.944795% | +1.840863% | +0.905261% | No |
+| 2.40° | +3.365834% | +8.866177% | +1.448816% | **Yes, acceleration** |
+| 3.32° | +11.506880% | +38.291632% | −2.028159% | **Yes, both ceilings** |
+
+The larger ceiling is not the same thing as a better operating point. At `3.32°`, the first fixed point is only `9.840072 Mbit/J`, versus `13.430253 Mbit/J` at `1.66°`; its served-PHY and rate-target counts also fall. The `2.40°` point is the only sampled wider point that combines higher absolute first/joint EE and higher fixed-point/joint target attainment with a materially larger acceleration ceiling.
+
+## Verified by running code
+
+Pooled EE is `sum(bits) / sum(joules)`. The numerator is the declaration-v1.8 item-5 saturated, full-buffer successfully decoded forward-downlink information bits, with **no demand cap**. Bits and joules are pooled across the same 12 development anchors and all 48 realised boundaries. Served PHY means positive realised decoding time. Rate-target attainment is separately counted and is not folded into EE.
+
+| Width | Construction | Pooled bits (Tbit) | Pooled joules | Pooled EE (Mbit/J) | Served PHY | Rate target attained |
+|---:|:---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| 1.66° | legal base | 1.121297 | 101679.495970 | 11.027760 | 960/1200 | 127/1200 |
+| 1.66° | first-improvement incumbent at 10 s | 1.248807 | 94696.380210 | 13.187489 | 1034/1200 | 137/1200 |
+| 1.66° | certified first-improvement fixed point | 1.255889 | 93511.944732 | 13.430253 | 1036/1200 | 147/1200 |
+| 1.66° | certified best-improvement fixed point | 1.249936 | 93911.175144 | 13.309765 | 1036/1200 | 146/1200 |
+| 1.66° | bounded joint selector from first fixed point | 1.267555 | 92580.086399 | 13.691444 | 1044/1200 | 149/1200 |
+| 2.40° | legal base | 1.059479 | 104625.380662 | 10.126402 | 961/1200 | 105/1200 |
+| 2.40° | first-improvement incumbent at 10 s | 1.223558 | 96213.073900 | 12.717166 | 1054/1200 | 126/1200 |
+| 2.40° | certified first-improvement fixed point | 1.264124 | 91307.457846 | 13.844693 | 1063/1200 | 176/1200 |
+| 2.40° | certified best-improvement fixed point | 1.257665 | 92157.088157 | 13.646973 | 1063/1200 | 175/1200 |
+| 2.40° | bounded joint selector from first fixed point | 1.283807 | 89709.671779 | 14.310682 | 1075/1200 | 180/1200 |
+| 3.32° | legal base | 0.756303 | 126649.276856 | 5.971635 | 742/1200 | 56/1200 |
+| 3.32° | first-improvement incumbent at 10 s | 0.902859 | 126887.139073 | 7.115450 | 859/1200 | 63/1200 |
+| 3.32° | certified first-improvement fixed point | 1.073439 | 109088.500083 | 9.840072 | 950/1200 | 128/1200 |
+| 3.32° | certified best-improvement fixed point | 1.086444 | 108170.832382 | 10.043776 | 950/1200 | 139/1200 |
+| 3.32° | bounded joint selector from first fixed point | 1.152580 | 105043.990322 | 10.972357 | 1006/1200 | 133/1200 |
+
+All three widths produced valid physics and complete results for 12/12 anchors. All 36 first-improvement and all 36 best-improvement terminal certificates are complete. The bounded joint catalogue was exhausted at every anchor: 8,864 unique configurations at `1.66°`, 7,237 at `2.40°`, and 9,122 at `3.32°`. A strict joint selection improvement occurred on 4/12, 7/12, and 9/12 anchors, respectively; the seed remained eligible.
+
+### Rate target, served PHY, and RF-cap binding
+
+The all-construction table above gives the requested rate-target and served-PHY counts. A wider beam does **not** monotonically relieve the target or cap constraint:
+
+- `2.40°` improves the fixed-point rate-target count from `147/1200` to `176/1200` and joint count from `149/1200` to `180/1200`; served PHY also rises from `1036` to `1063` at the first fixed point.
+- `3.32°` reverses that improvement: first-fixed attainment is `128/1200`, joint attainment is `133/1200`, and first-fixed served PHY is `950/1200`, all below the `1.66°` control.
+
+The cited v2 finding that `nominal_required_power_over_cap == 1.0` on every corpus row applies to action-row observations. To compare widths without changing scope mid-grid, I separately replayed the five selected constructions at nominal boundary 0: 12 anchors × 5 constructions = 60 configuration-anchor profiles per width. This is a selected-profile cap audit, **not** a reproduction of the full action-row corpus audit.
+
+| Width | Exact-one required-power profiles | Base | Actual 10 s | First fixed | Best fixed | Joint from first | Ratio range | First-fixed boundary-0 RF cap-hit share |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|:---|---:|
+| 1.66° | 53/60 | 12/12 | 11/12 | 10/12 | 10/12 | 10/12 | 0.329020–1.000000 | 17.089651% |
+| 2.40° | 49/60 | 12/12 | 12/12 | 8/12 | 9/12 | 8/12 | 0.178267–1.000000 | 11.351196% |
+| 3.32° | 55/60 | 12/12 | 12/12 | 11/12 | 9/12 | 11/12 | 0.179457–1.000000 | 17.922044% |
+
+Thus `2.40°` provides partial selected-profile cap relief, but not universal relief: every base and every actual-10-second profile is still at an exact-one network maximum. At `3.32°`, exact-one profiles rise above the control count and the first-fixed RF cap-hit share rises above the control. The endpoint therefore does not relieve the RF-cap regime overall.
+
+### Physics-regeneration cost and resource envelope
+
+PANELCEIL constructs one complete 33-step detached tape per width because its anchor observations include the declared future projections, then uses the frozen 12-anchor prefix. “Seconds per anchor” below is the measured tape-construction wall time divided by 12; it excludes search and committed evaluation.
+
+| Width | Tape regeneration (s) | Physics seconds/anchor | Peak RSS, main sweep | Result |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|:---|
+| 1.66° | 263.625947 | 21.968829 | 2.524 GiB | 12/12 valid |
+| 2.40° | 271.965509 | 22.663792 | 2.319 GiB | 12/12 valid |
+| 3.32° | 327.475859 | 27.289655 | 2.394 GiB | 12/12 valid |
+
+Each main width and cap audit ran serially in one Python process under `nice -n 15`, with `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS`, `NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS`, `VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS`, and `BLIS_NUM_THREADS` all set to `1`. A 4.9 GB RSS watchdog plus PANELCEIL's own `<5,000,000,000`-byte assertion was active. The largest recorded main-sweep peak was 2,709,970,944 bytes. The only interpreter used was `/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover/.venv/bin/python`.
+
+An initial control attempt used a 4.9 GB address-space limit and stopped after 7 anchors when NumPy could not reserve a 1.22 MiB array despite RSS being only 1.654 GiB. Address space is not RSS; the partial receipt was resumed after replacing that unsuitable limit with the RSS watchdog. No reported metric came from a failed or partial width.
+
+## Derived on paper from verified aggregates
+
+The three quantities use only the pooled EEs above:
+
+- coordination ceiling = `EE(J_FROM_FIRST) / EE(U_FIRST) - 1`;
+- acceleration ceiling = `EE(U_FIRST) / EE(U_FIRST_AT_10) - 1`;
+- traversal-order gap = `EE(U_FIRST) / EE(U_BEST) - 1`.
+
+For example, at `3.32°`, `10.9723570805 / 9.8400718422 - 1 = +11.506880%`, and `9.8400718422 / 7.1154499154 - 1 = +38.291632%`.
+
+The untimed `1.66°` constructions reproduce the standing PANELCEIL aggregates exactly to the stored binary64 values: base, both fixed points, joint-from-first, coordination `+1.944795%`, and traversal gap `+0.905261%`. The fresh actual-10-second control is `13.187489 Mbit/J`, so this run's acceleration ceiling is `+1.840863%`, not the earlier `+1.045609%` obtained when the same search completed more moves within ten wall-clock seconds.
+
+Relative to the control, absolute first-fixed EE changes by `+3.085867%` at `2.40°` and `−26.732043%` at `3.32°`; joint EE changes by `+4.522812%` and `−19.859751%`, respectively. These comparisons are descriptive design-phase arithmetic, not selection criteria.
+
+## Inferred
+
+- **The ceiling question is answered yes.** The conclusion does not depend only on the wall-clock checkpoint: the deterministic coordination ceiling itself reaches `+11.506880%` at `3.32°`.
+- The large acceleration ceilings are partly computational. Wider-beam search paths took much longer on several anchors, while the 10-second construction credits only moves whose evaluations completed by the checkpoint. These are valid measurements on this machine under the required live-load conditions, but their exact percentages should not be assumed hardware- or load-invariant.
+- `3.32°` creates coordination headroom but degrades absolute EE, served PHY, rate attainment, and the selected-profile cap census. It is therefore evidence for a larger optimization opportunity, not by itself evidence that `3.32°` is the better operating point.
+- `2.40°` is the more plausible sampled design candidate: it raises absolute first/joint EE, served PHY, and fixed/joint target attainment while exposing `+8.866177%` acceleration headroom. Its deterministic coordination headroom remains only `+3.365834%`, so whether that is worth a learning program is a design-value judgment, not established here.
+- The negative `−2.028159%` traversal gap at `3.32°` means best-improvement finishes above first-improvement there. Traversal order remains consequential, but its direction is not stable over the grid.
+
+## Construction and provenance
+
+The declared grid, stated before execution and never changed, was `1.66°, 2.40°, 3.32°`. The frozen development panel is `V025_PROBE/world/1`, steps 0–3, crossed in declared order with `nearest-eligible`, `stay-if-possible`, and `random-masked` (12 anchors, 100 users each). No evaluation-only claim date was opened.
+
+The hash-pinned PANELCEIL runner was loaded read-only from `/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling30-ws/.scratch/panelceil/run_panelceil.py`, SHA-256 `dda958d166c42d276704a696e20966ac4236b78e4f88ecf5120e8a73ef817f95`. A local adapter set `mcrl.physics_v025.channel.TX_FULL_HPBW_DEG = 2 × one-sided width` in memory before each new `build_world_tape`; no sealed source or constant was edited. The distinct tape digests at the two non-control widths verify that physics was regenerated rather than reused.
+
+Traversal names, always:
+
+- first improvement: **ascending user ID; declared legal-option order; first strict guarded-`F` improvement in ordered batches of 16**, cycled to a complete no-move certificate;
+- best improvement: **ascending user ID; declared legal-option order in batches of 16; exact best strict response per user; stable configuration-ID tie-break**, cycled to a complete no-move certificate.
+
+Both start from the same legal base and use exact `F = decoded bits − eta_ref × joules` with the unchanged base-served guard. The 10-second incumbent is the last first-improvement move whose evaluation completed by 10 seconds. The joint selector is reseeded from the certified first fixed point and exhausts the unchanged bounded catalogue: same-beam occupant subsets of size 2–4 with the 1,024-generation cap, victim plus top-2/top-3 physical nominal contributors, complete-beam evacuations, stable assignment order, batches of 32, and the 4,096 unique-candidate cap.
+
+## Machine-readable evidence
+
